-- title:
--   Relabelling automorphisms act linearly on Drinfeld origin parameters
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a nonzero natural number $M'$, and a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$, and let $A_0$ be a commutative ring in which $\ell_g$ is invertible (`hℓA`).
--
--   **Level-transport hypotheses.** Three hypotheses state that the three level structures are stable under variable change over an arbitrary commutative $A_0$-algebra $T$: `hℓ` says that if a quadruple $D = (x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q)$ satisfies `IsGamma1Point W ℓg D` (the affine equation of $W$ holds at $(x_P,y_P)$, $(\mathrm{pre}\Psi_{\ell_g})(x_P) = 0$, and $x_Q = x_P$, $y_Q = y_P$), then `D.variableChange C` satisfies it for $C \bullet W$; `hM` says that `IsGamma0PowAt W p k h` (for $p^k = 2$, the `IsTwoKernel` conditions on $h$, and otherwise the `IsCyclicGenKernel` conditions: degree at most $\varphi(p^k)/2$, coefficient $1$ in that degree, $h \cdot \mathrm{pre}\Psi_{p^{k-1}} \mid \mathrm{pre}\Psi_{p^k}$, and the divisibility of the relevant `smulNumerator`s) is carried to `IsGamma0PowAt (C • W) p k (kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h)`; and `hL` says that a divisor $h$ of `inLineMulPoly W ℓg n x` has `kernelVariableChangeDeg C d h` dividing `inLineMulPoly (C • W) ℓg n (u_C^{-2}(x - r_C))`.
--
--   **Group-law and transport hypotheses.** $\mathcal{G}$ is a family of relative group laws on the graded projective models of Weierstrass curves with unit discriminant over $A_0$-algebras, assumed chord–tangent (`h𝒢`: each law admits a points-evaluation) and origin-normalised (`h𝒢O`: for each such curve there is a ring homomorphism $\chi$ from the origin chart ring realising the identity section as an origin-chart section with $\chi(x/y) = \chi(z/y) = 0$); $\mathcal{T}$ is a `LevelTransport` for $\mathcal{G}$ at $q$, assumed to be a section transport (`h𝒯`: its `act` and `map` operations move the two sections of a raw Drinfeld pair compatibly with variable-change and coefficient homomorphisms of the projective models). The hypotheses `hVC` and `hCO` assert the existence, for every curve, variable change $C$ and $A_0$-algebra map $f$, of graded ring homomorphisms of the projective-model graded rings satisfying `IsVariableChangeHom`, respectively `IsCoefficientHom`, together with the comparison of irrelevant ideals.
--
--   **Moduli package.** Let $P_0$ be a fine moduli package for the level moduli datum attached to `rigidDataH1Pow A₀ ℓg M' q hℓ hM hL 𝒢 𝒯` — the rigid Weierstrass datum whose raw objects over $T$ consist of a curve with unit discriminant together with a triple of a $\Gamma_0$-power kernel datum, a $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point datum and a Drinfeld pair of level $q$, subject to the link condition `IsGamma1Link`; thus $P_0$ provides a ring $B_0$ with an $A_0$-algebra structure, a point `P₀.univ` of the quotient functor, and the universal property that every point over $T$ is `D.map φ P₀.univ` for a unique $A_0$-algebra map $\varphi : B_0 \to T$, whence the classifying maps `P₀.classify`. Let $x$ be a raw object over $B_0$ whose class is `P₀.univ` (`hx`).
--
--   **Coefficient and deformation hypotheses.** $R$ is a noetherian local domain, complete for its maximal ideal and an $A_0$-algebra, with $\iota : B_0 \to R$ an $A_0$-algebra map; $k$ is a field of characteristic $q$ in which $\ell_g$ and $M'$ are nonzero (`hℓk`, `hM'k`), and $\mathrm{res}_R : R \to k$ is a surjection with kernel the maximal ideal of $R$. $W_0$ is a complete discrete valuation domain with maximal ideal generated by $q$ and a surjection $\mathrm{res}_0 : W_0 \to k$ with kernel that maximal ideal; $R$ is a $W_0$-algebra, $W_0$ an $A_0$-algebra, the tower is compatible, and $\mathrm{res}_R$ restricted to $W_0$ is $\mathrm{res}_0$ (`hresR₀`). The hypothesis `hfac` states that for every artinian local $W_0$- and $A_0$-algebra $T$ in this tower, equipped with a surjection $\mathrm{res}_T : T \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal and restricting to $\mathrm{res}_0$ on $W_0$, every $A_0$-algebra map $\varphi : B_0 \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \varphi = \mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$ extends to a unique $W_0$-algebra map $\Phi : R \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \Phi = \mathrm{res}_R$ and $\Phi \circ \iota = \varphi$.
--
--   **Formal group hypotheses.** $F$ is a commutative formal group over $R$ whose power series is the fixed formal group law `formalGroupLawFixed` of the curve carried by the Drinfeld-pair component of the raw object obtained from $x$ by base change along $\iota$ (`hFW`), and $F_0$ is a commutative formal group over $k$ whose power series is the corresponding law for the reduction of that curve along $\mathrm{res}_R$ (`hF₀W`), with $F$ a base change of $F_0$ in the sense `F.IsBaseChange resR F₀` (`hBC`). Writing $\chi_P, \chi_Q$ for two ring homomorphisms from the origin chart ring of that curve to $R$, with origin parameters `originParam χP`, `originParam χQ`, the hypotheses `hP` and `hQ` state that $\chi_P$, respectively $\chi_Q$, is an origin-chart section for the section $P$, respectively $Q$, of the Drinfeld pair and that its origin parameter and its `originW` lie in the maximal ideal of $R$. Further, `hF₀` states that for the zero ideal the $q$-th series of $F_0$ is a unit multiple of the Drinfeld divisor at $(0,0)$; `hDr` states the same for $F$ relative to the maximal ideal of $R$ and the pair of origin parameters; and `hmax` states that the maximal ideal of $R$ is generated by these two origin parameters. The hypothesis `huniv` is the universal property of this Drinfeld-basis deformation: for every artinian local $W_0$-algebra $T$ with a surjection $\mathrm{res}_T$ to $k$ having kernel the maximal ideal and restricting to $\mathrm{res}_0$, every commutative formal group $G$ over $T$ which is a base change of $F_0$ along $\mathrm{res}_T$, and all $y_0, y_1$ in the maximal ideal of $T$ forming a Drinfeld basis of level $q$ for $G$ adically, there is a unique $W_0$-algebra map $\varphi : R \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \varphi = \mathrm{res}_R$ for which there exist a formal group $F'$ over $T$ with $F$ base-changing to $F'$ along $\varphi$ and an isomorphism of laws $\psi : F' \to G$ whose series has all coefficients reducing to those of the identity series (coefficient $n$ mapping to $1$ if $n = 1$ and to $0$ otherwise) and which sends $\varphi(\mathrm{originParam}\,\chi_P)$ to $y_0$ and $\varphi(\mathrm{originParam}\,\chi_Q)$ to $y_1$ under `appAdic` for the maximal ideal of $T$.
--
--   **Supersingularity.** The hypothesis `hssJ` requires that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\iota_\Omega : k \to \Omega$, the image of $\mathrm{res}_R(\iota(\mathtt{P₀.j₀}))$ under $\iota_\Omega$ lies in `ssJSet q Ω`, that is: every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero point killed by $q$.
--
--   **Relabelling data.** Let $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ lie in $\Gamma_0(M')$ and let $\rho_\gamma$ be an automorphism of the moduli problem (a natural self-map of the point functor preserving the $j$-invariant). The pinning hypothesis `hpin` states that over any field $T$ with an $A_0$-algebra structure, and for raw objects $y, y'$ over $T$ with $y$'s Drinfeld-pair curve having unit discriminant, if $y'$ has the same curve as $y$, the same $\Gamma_0$-power component, its $\Gamma_1$-point is $\gamma_{00}$ times the point of $y$ (both formed by `LevelRelabelling.toPoint` on the base change of the curve), its $Q$-coordinates coincide with its $P$-coordinates, and its Drinfeld pair is `RawDrinfeldPair.relabel 𝒢 γ` applied to that of $y$, then $\rho_\gamma$ carries the class of $y$ to the class of $y'$. Finally, `hfix` states that $\iota(\mathtt{P₀.classify}(\rho_\gamma \cdot \mathtt{P₀.univ})(b)) - \iota(b)$ lies in the maximal ideal of $R$ for every $b \in B_0$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses there exist a ring isomorphism $\theta_0 : R \to R$ and an element $c \in R$ such that, writing $x_P = \mathrm{originParam}\,\chi_P$ and $x_Q = \mathrm{originParam}\,\chi_Q$ and $\mathfrak{m}$ for the maximal ideal of $R$:
--
--   1. $\theta_0(\iota(b)) = \iota(\mathtt{P₀.classify}(\rho_\gamma \cdot \mathtt{P₀.univ})(b))$ for every $b \in B_0$;
--
--   2. $\theta_0$ fixes $\mathrm{algebraMap}_{W_0 \to R}(w)$ for every $w \in W_0$;
--
--   3. $\theta_0(r) - r \in \mathfrak{m}$ for every $r \in R$;
--
--   4. $\theta_0(x_P) - c\,(\gamma_{00} x_P + \gamma_{10} x_Q) \in \mathfrak{m}^2$, the matrix entries being taken as integers mapped into $R$;
--
--   5. $\theta_0(x_Q) - c\,(\gamma_{01} x_P + \gamma_{11} x_Q) \in \mathfrak{m}^2$;
--
--   6. $c^{q+1} - 1 \in \mathfrak{m}$;
--
--   7. if the image of $\gamma_{11}$ in $\mathbb{Z}/\ell_g$ is $1$, then $c - 1 \in \mathfrak{m}$;
--
--   8. if the image of $\gamma_{11}$ in $\mathbb{Z}/\ell_g$ is $-1$, then $c + 1 \in \mathfrak{m}$;
--
--   9. if $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and the image of $\gamma_{11}$ in $\mathbb{Z}/\ell_g$ is not $1$, and moreover that image is not $-1$ in case $q = 2$, then $c - 1 \notin \mathfrak{m}$.
--
--   This is the equivariance computation at a supersingular point of the $\Gamma_0(M') \times \Gamma_1(\ell_g) \times$ Drinfeld-$\Gamma(q)$ moduli problem: a relabelling automorphism coming from $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ is realised by an automorphism of the complete local deformation ring which acts on the two Drinfeld origin parameters, modulo the square of the maximal ideal, by $\gamma$ scaled by a single constant $c$ satisfying $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$, with $c \equiv \pm 1$ governed by the diamond character at $\ell_g$ and with $c \not\equiv 1$ off that locus. It is the input to the statement producing, for these data, a regular local adic completion carrying a Drinfeld basis, a Hasse parameter and the induced linear part of the relabelling automorphism.
